WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:07.120
Vsauce! Kevin here. And I’m about to bust the&nbsp;
greatest female tycoon of the 19th century using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:13.360
FORENSIC MATH -- I just need skim milk, an&nbsp;
amputated leg, and a family of nerds to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:29.840
it. Millions of dollars were at stake&nbsp;
-- and it all came down to handwriting.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:37.840
I’ve told some stories about how math&nbsp;
has perverted the pursuit of justice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:45.040
and destroyed lives -- but when one of America’s&nbsp;
richest women tried to steal even more money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.480
math detected her fraud.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.840
Hetty Green was bred to make money&nbsp;
-- and she was the best at it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.080
At 6 years old, Hetty was reading&nbsp;
financial newspapers to her father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:04.320
who owned a whaling firm in Massachusetts. She&nbsp;
tracked stock prices with her grandfather, and by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:11.760
age 13 was the family bookkeeper. In the 1850’s,&nbsp;
the family had MILLIONS -- which in today’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:20.000
money is TENS of millions. But Hetty needed more.&nbsp;
And when her aunt died, she saw dollar signs.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:27.280
First, you need to understand Hetty. Her&nbsp;
entire LIFE was all about money -- and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.240
she racked up a fortune that would be&nbsp;
valued well into the BILLIONS today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:39.280
She was known as the “Witch of Wall Street”&nbsp;
-- but she was famously miserly. She was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:44.480
as obsessed with making money as she&nbsp;
was with never spending ANY of it.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:45.920
Here are some examples.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:53.360
She spent so long trying to find a free clinic&nbsp;
to treat her son’s leg injury that she waited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.360 --> 00:02:00.240
too long and it had to be amputated. She refused&nbsp;
to use hot water because fuel to heat it felt like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:07.600
a waste of money. She wore one black dress and a&nbsp;
single set of underwear that she only changed when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.640
they wore out. When she bailed out the New York&nbsp;
City government by writing a check to cover its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:19.680
budgetary shortfalls, she was living in boarding&nbsp;
houses -- because she wanted to. She said, “Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:27.200
because I dress plainly and do not spend a fortune&nbsp;
on my gowns, they say I am cranky or insane.”

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:35.200
Hetty Green wasn’t insane -- but she was a&nbsp;
criminal. And that’s where the math comes in.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.480
When her aunt Sylvia died in 1865,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:45.120
her will stipulated that about $1 million would&nbsp;
go to charities. That’s about $15 million today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:52.240
The other $1 million would be put in a trust&nbsp;
for Hetty. So half for charity. Half for Hetty.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.000
Here’s what happened next...

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.480
Hetty remarkably produced an earlier&nbsp;
copy of the will stating that 100%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:08.240
of her aunt’s fortune would go directly to her…&nbsp;
which seemed pretty convenient. It also had a page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:15.680
attached that invalidated any future wills, which&nbsp;
seemed… really convenient. The estate’s executor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.680
Thomas Mandell, saw it and&nbsp;
was like, “I don’t think so.”

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:25.440
So what did Hetty do? She sued the estate&nbsp;
-- and that’s when nerds came to the rescue.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:33.280
Charles Sanders Peirce was a literal genius --&nbsp;
by age 23, he had 3 degrees from Harvard. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:40.000
eventually became one of America’s greatest&nbsp;
logicians, including realizing in 1886 that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:47.280
electrical circuits could carry out logical&nbsp;
operations. That resulted in the digital computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:56.240
decades later, but in 1865, he entered a courtroom&nbsp;
in Boston to analyze Aunt Sylvia’s handwriting.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:01.280
Charles compared 42 samples of&nbsp;
Sylvia’s signature and concluded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:07.520
that it had 30 downstrokes -- in graphonomics,&nbsp;
a “ballistic stroke” that conveys a distinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:16.000
change in the absolute velocity of the pen. My&nbsp;
signature has 18 -- 8 in Kevin, 10 in Lieber.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:21.840
Here’s a question. How consistent is a&nbsp;
signature? How much did Sylvia’s vary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:25.680
from sample to sample? How much does MINE vary?

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.920
LET’S FIND OUT. Right after this.

