00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:22.720
Okay. I signed my name, and then I made 42 more&nbsp;
signatures -- and tried to make them match.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:28.640
Then I judged each sample against the original&nbsp;
signature to find out how many of the 18&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:34.800
downstrokes in my name were the same -- I looked&nbsp;
at size, length, and angle to determine whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:41.040
each stroke was substantially similar. They&nbsp;
followed a normal distribution, and the result was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:50.320
an average of 7.405 matches out of a possible 18&nbsp;
-- so only 41% the same. And I was really trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:56.000
to make them the same! Sylvia’s 42 signatures&nbsp;
were probably done over a period of years.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:03.280
But the interesting thing is the variance -- my&nbsp;
signatures have a standard deviation of 1.326,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:10.960
which means that 99.73% of my&nbsp;
signatures should fall between 3.427&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:18.320
and 11.383 -- that’s a range of plus and&nbsp;
minus 3 sigma. The Z-score of each sample&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:23.440
indicates how many standard deviations&nbsp;
that sample is from the mean -- basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:32.480
how much of an outlier it is. My most deviant&nbsp;
sample was 10 matches, for a Z-score of 1.957.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:36.400
I actually did one more set of&nbsp;
comparisons just to make sure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:42.640
I compared signature #21 to all the&nbsp;
others. The mean was 6.810 matches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:50.560
and the standard deviation was 1.418.&nbsp;
The highest Z score was still only 2.249.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:56.080
Based on my 42 samples, how deviant&nbsp;
would a perfect 18-for-18 match&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:05.440
actually be? It would have a Z-score of 7.989&nbsp;
-- nearly 8 standard deviations from the mean.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:09.360
The odds of a signature being seven&nbsp;
standard deviations from the mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:19.040
are about 1 in 390 billion -- the odds of 7.989?&nbsp;
Uhhh. Figure it out and tell me in the comments.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:19.760
Back to Hetty.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:26.400
Charles Peirce found that Sylvia’s 42 samples&nbsp;
had an average of 6 downward strokes matching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:32.240
the will’s signature -- so about 20% --&nbsp;
and conformed to a binomial distribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:35.600
just like mine. And probably YOURS.

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:40.400
When Charles compared the signature on the&nbsp;
first page of the will to the signature on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:51.200
the attached second page, he found that all 30&nbsp;
downstrokes were a perfect match. A 100% perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:56.560
match. His father Benjamin Peirce was&nbsp;
a Harvard mathematician -- he testified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:01.520
that the probability of Sylvia’s&nbsp;
second signature being a perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:17.680
30 for 30 match was 2.666 x 10^21 -- or 1&nbsp;
in 2 sextillion, 666 quintillion. So yeah.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:23.520
Benjamin Peirce said, “Such evanescent&nbsp;
shadows of probability cannot belong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:32.240
to actual life.” Which is an amazing quote.&nbsp;
Uhhh yeah, Hetty traced Aunt Sylvia’s signature.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:37.360
She lost the case and fled with her husband&nbsp;
to London when the family threatened to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:43.680
her indicted for fraud. But… she didn’t&nbsp;
really lose. After 5 years in court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:51.360
she was awarded $600,000 from the estate -- less&nbsp;
than the original legitimate will gave her, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:59.120
still $12 million in today’s money. And the state&nbsp;
never brought criminal charges against... the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:07.120
wealthiest woman living in it. The following year,&nbsp;
Hetty had a daughter -- who she named Sylvia.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:15.040
Hetty Green died during a heated argument with her&nbsp;
maid about the benefits of skim milk. Today her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:22.960
estate would be valued at $5 billion. She really&nbsp;
was America’s first female business tycoon -- and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:29.040
she wasn’t all bad. She became an anonymous&nbsp;
philanthropist and served as a nurse to care for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:40.000
friends and family. In 1951, Sylvia Green died,&nbsp;
leaving over 99% of the family fortune to charity.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:46.320
I’ve made videos about the crazy odds of two&nbsp;
people having the same name -- and having weirdly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:53.680
similar fingerprints -- and having rare tragedies&nbsp;
strike twice in the same family. I’ve shown that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.680 --> 00:10:01.520
math alone is a terrible basis for convicting&nbsp;
someone of a crime -- and insane odds can still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:08.400
mean innocence. With Sylvia Ann Howland’s&nbsp;
will, we have to consider the totality of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:15.600
the circumstances: Hetty’s motive, her obsessive&nbsp;
greed, her miraculous production of a will that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:25.040
just happened to be entirely in her favor -- all&nbsp;
combined with truly astronomical signature odds.

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:31.200
Knowing when not to believe in coincidences&nbsp;
is just as crucial as accepting them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:35.680
1 in a billion? Okay -- if 1 in&nbsp;
a billion people won a lottery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:40.800
that means about 8 people alive&nbsp;
right now would hit the jackpot. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:50.080
not impossible. A mountain of evidence plus 1 in&nbsp;
2.6 sextillion? Yeah. I don’t think so, Hetty.

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:54.160
Hetty Green spent a lifetime accumulating money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.160 --> 00:11:00.160
power, and influence -- and she could never&nbsp;
get enough of any of it. But in the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:07.520
all of Hetty’s precious millions couldn’t&nbsp;
compete with the Peirce family’s sextillions.

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:13.840
And as always, thanks for watching.

